WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:07.656
A Kafka reader hands payments to a writer that inserts them into Oracle.

2
00:00:07.656 --> 00:00:08.964
The reader is fast.

3
00:00:08.964 --> 00:00:10.272
The writer is slow.

4
00:00:10.500 --> 00:00:12.827
Put an unbounded queue between them.

5
00:00:12.827 --> 00:00:14.055
What happens to it?

6
00:00:15.533 --> 00:00:16.419
It only grows.

7
00:00:16.419 --> 00:00:21.797
Every payment the writer can't keep up with waits in memory, until the heap runs out.

8
00:00:22.133 --> 00:00:23.580
So bound the queue.

9
00:00:23.580 --> 00:00:25.027
Give it five slots.

10
00:00:25.233 --> 00:00:27.720
The queue is full, so the next put waits.

11
00:00:27.720 --> 00:00:30.388
The reader stops until the writer takes one.

12
00:00:30.667 --> 00:00:35.260
The slow side now pushes back on the fast side, instead of filling memory.

13
00:00:35.500 --> 00:00:36.933
A second writer joins.

14
00:00:36.933 --> 00:00:39.537
Then the reader finishes its last batch.

15
00:00:39.537 --> 00:00:41.621
Three payments are still queued.

16
00:00:41.833 --> 00:00:43.516
The writers drain them.

17
00:00:43.516 --> 00:00:44.833
Then what happens?

18
00:00:46.333 --> 00:00:48.073
They wait in take, forever.

19
00:00:48.073 --> 00:00:51.423
Only a put wakes a take, and nothing will put again.

20
00:00:51.423 --> 00:00:52.647
The shutdown hangs.

21
00:00:52.867 --> 00:00:55.424
Rewind to the moment the reader finished.

22
00:00:55.424 --> 00:01:00.539
This time, a coordinator puts one poison pill per writer, behind the last payment.

23
00:01:00.767 --> 00:01:04.788
Each writer takes payments until it takes a pill, then stops.

24
00:01:04.788 --> 00:01:08.545
No payment is left behind, and no writer is left waiting.

25
00:01:08.767 --> 00:01:11.376
What if the coordinator puts only one pill?

26
00:01:12.867 --> 00:01:14.078
One writer stops.

27
00:01:14.078 --> 00:01:16.357
The other waits in take forever.

28
00:01:17.367 --> 00:01:21.656
In code, each writer loops on take and breaks at the pill.

29
00:01:21.656 --> 00:01:29.348
Compare by identity, with == in Java and === in Kotlin, so a payment that reads PILL is still a payment.

30
00:01:29.567 --> 00:01:34.331
The coordinator puts the pills after joining every producer, one per writer.

31
00:01:34.533 --> 00:01:41.665
The rule: bound the queue so the fast side waits, and stop with one pill per consumer, after the last producer.

32
00:01:43.367 --> 00:01:44.166
Quick check.

33
00:01:44.166 --> 00:01:47.499
The pills go in while a producer is still running.

34
00:01:47.499 --> 00:01:48.699
What can go wrong?

35
00:01:50.867 --> 00:01:52.313
The writers stop early.

36
00:01:52.313 --> 00:01:56.085
Then the queue fills, and the producer waits in put forever.
